## a message that was never sent cannot receive another one
##
## 'a:b' folds into a single item as it is read - a receiver bound to a message,
## not yet sent. Binding a further message to that would leave the order of the
## two sends unsaid, so it is refused, and the message names a spelling that
## says it: (a:b):c, or equally a:b():c.

(a:b:c) ##ERROR: syntax error - bound message not usable as a receiver - send it first, as in (a:b):c

---

## the gap around ':' does not enter into it. what is refused is the fold, not
## a way of writing it

(a : b : c) ##ERROR: syntax error - bound message not usable as a receiver - send it first, as in (a:b):c

---

## the arguments that would have followed make no difference either - the
## receiver is settled before them

(a:b:c 1 2) ##ERROR: syntax error - bound message not usable as a receiver - send it first, as in (a:b):c

---

## at statement level, where the enclosing parenthesis is the one hak supplies

a:b:c ##ERROR: syntax error - bound message not usable as a receiver - send it first, as in (a:b):c

---

## a send that HAS been completed is a perfectly good receiver, so only the
## trailing ':d' is refused here - 'a:b():c' on its own is allowed

(a:b():c:d) ##ERROR: syntax error - bound message not usable as a receiver - send it first, as in (a:b):c

---

## a bound message that nothing sends
##
## A send takes its arguments from a glued list - x:greet() - or from the list
## it heads - (x:greet). This one heads neither, so it reaches the compiler in
## a value position with no way to run. Both spellings are named.

printf "%O\n" x:greet ##ERROR: syntax error - message 'greet' bound but not sent - write (x:greet) or x:greet()

---

## the same on the right of an assignment

r := x:greet ##ERROR: syntax error - message 'greet' bound but not sent - write (x:greet) or x:greet()

---

## and as an element of an array literal, whose elements are compiled

a := #[x:greet] ##ERROR: syntax error - message 'greet' bound but not sent - write (x:greet) or x:greet()

---

## when the receiver is itself a computed expression it has no token text, so
## it is described by shape rather than quoted as nothing

printf "%O\n" (x:greet()):hello ##ERROR: syntax error - message 'hello' bound but not sent - write (( ):hello) or ( ):hello()

---

## a send that already carries its own argument list, wrapped in parentheses
##
## (Maker:make(4)) calls what the send ANSWERED - the ordinary meaning of
## (f(1)) - so the function runs with no arguments and complains about the one
## it wanted. The send was already complete without them; write
## Maker:make(4)(9), or (Maker:make 4)(9).
##
## This one is here rather than in t/send-01.hak because an argument-count
## mismatch on a function block is not catchable - try/catch does not see it
## and the script ends.

class Maker {
	fun[#c] make(x) { return (fun(j) { return (+ (* x 10) j) }) }
}

(Maker:make(4))(9) ##ERROR: wrong number of argument passed to function block - 1 expected, 0 passed
